<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Qatna</h1><p class="st-meta">2000 BCE – 1340 BCE (approx.) · capital Qatna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Qatna, a wealthy Bronze Age kingdom near Homs whose intact royal tomb and palace show Syria's prosperity between Egypt, Mitanni and the Hittites.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Qatna, at Tell el-Mishrifeh, was refounded at the start of the 2nd millennium BCE inside huge square earthen ramparts. Its Amorite kings controlled central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> to the coast, and became the main rivals of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yamhad">Yamhad</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> era King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishhi-addu">Ishhi-Addu</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a> and married his daughter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasmah-addu">Yasmah-Addu</a>. The royal palace held a hypogeum, found unplundered in 2002, with gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and the bones of generations of the royal family. Later kings left Akkadian archives with many Hurrian words.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 14th century BCE Qatna lay between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>. Its king Akizzi appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, but Hittite forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a> sacked the city c. 1340 BCE and it never recovered.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/qatna.jpg" alt="Qatna" width="320" height="312"><figcaption>Image: Attar-Aram syria · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qatna%27s_landmarks.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2000 BCE – 1340 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Qatna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ishhi-Addu, Amut-pi-el, Akizzi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Syrian Semitic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Amorite, Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Qatna, Tell el-Mishrifeh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1670&amp;focus=qatna" aria-label="Qatna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5615 917 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Qatna lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5935 1173L5996 1079M5935 1173L5863 1489M5935 1173L5852 1002"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5996 1079h0M5863 1489h0M5852 1002h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5935 1173h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5935 1173h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1670&amp;focus=qatna">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

